<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Provostry of Berchtesgaden</h1><p class="st-meta">1380 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Berchtesgaden</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Alpine church principality of Augustinian canons whose salt mine made it a coveted little state between Bavaria and Salzburg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Augustinian canons settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a> early in the 12th century, endowed by the family of the counts of Sulzbach. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> granted the provost rights over forests and salt in 1156, and in 1380 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iv-of-bohemia">King Wenceslaus</a> confirmed the provostry's direct dependence on the empire; from 1559 its provosts were counted among the princes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The provost, elected by the canons, ruled a small mountain land enclosed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Its wealth came from salt, mined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a> salt mine from 1517, which caused long disputes with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-archbishopric-of-salzburg">Prince-Archbishopric of Salzburg</a> and Bavaria over the trade. Woodcarving became a famous local craft. For long periods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach</a> princes held the provostry alongside other sees.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Secularised in 1803, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a> went to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-salzburg">Electorate of Salzburg</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1805 and to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a> in 1810. In the 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berghof">Hitler's Berghof</a> stood on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obersalzberg">Obersalzberg</a> above the town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-provostry-of-berchtesgaden.jpg" alt="Prince-Provostry of Berchtesgaden" width="320" height="261"><figcaption>Image: kgberger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HRR_1789_F%C3%BCrstpropstei_Berchtesgaden.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1380 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Berchtesgaden</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ferdinand of Bavaria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstpropstei Berchtesgaden</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1592&amp;focus=prince-provostry-of-berchtesgaden" aria-label="Prince-Provostry of Berchtesgaden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4984 508 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Provostry of Berchtesgaden lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5304 764L5229 694M5304 764L5304 759"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5304 759h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 764h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 764h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1592&amp;focus=prince-provostry-of-berchtesgaden">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
